[Event "London System: the pyramid"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/6QUAuVXa"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today we learn the London System. The plan is a pyramid: the bishop comes out to f4 first, then the pawns c3, d4 and e3 build the wall behind it. Start with the queen pawn, two squares to d4. [%cal Yd2d4] }
1. d4 { Your pawn on d4 claims the centre. It looks solid, and the London plan hides a real attack behind it. } (1. e4 { The king pawn also grabs the centre, and it is just as sound a first move. It leads toward the Italian or the Ruy Lopez, not the London we want today. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { His knight develops, heading toward a completely different kind of game than the one we are building. }) (1. Nf3 { Flexible, and it often transposes back into a queen pawn game anyway. Today we play the pawn first, every time. } 1... d5 2. d4 Nf6 { He mirrors the centre push. This road and the London usually meet again a few moves later. }) (1. c4 { The English Opening, a fine choice in its own right and nothing like the London plan we are learning. } 1... e5 2. g3 Nf6 { His knight develops normally. A different opening entirely — we want the bishop out to f4 today. }) 1... d5 { He answers your pawn in kind. Now the move that names the whole opening: the dark-squared bishop out to f4. [%cal Yc1f4] } 2. Bf4 { Your bishop steps outside the pawn chain before the pawns can trap it at home. That is the London System. [%csl Gf4] } (2. c4 { The Queen's Gambit, one of the most respected replies to 1. d4 in all of chess. } 2... e6 3. Nf3 dxc4 { He grabs the pawn, and a real fight over the centre begins — rich chess, but not the London we came for today. }) (2. Nf3 { Also excellent, and it usually reaches a similar game. In the London the bishop leaves home before the e pawn does. } 2... Nf6 3. c4 dxc4 { He grabs the pawn here too. A different game again, since your bishop never got its trip to f4. }) (2. e3 { Playing the e pawn first shuts your own bishop in behind it, on c1. } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 e6 { He develops calmly. That trapped bishop on c1 is exactly what the London plan avoids. [%csl Rc1] }) (2. Nc3 { His knight sits on c3, the very square your own c pawn wants for the pyramid's third brick. } 2... c5 3. Bf4 Nc6 { You brought the bishop out anyway, but your c pawn now has nowhere useful to stand. }) 2... c5 { He strikes at your centre pawn with his own. Never capture on c5 — build the pyramid instead, starting with the e pawn, one square. [%cal Ye2e3] } 3. e3 { The first brick goes down. It also opens a door home for your king's bishop. [%csl Ge3] } (3. dxc5 { Capturing on c5 lets go of the centre tension for nothing — his bishop simply comes and sits there next move. } 3... e6 4. e3 Bxc5 { His bishop recaptures on c5 and now aims straight down at f2, and it cost him no time at all to get there. Leave that pawn alone instead. }) (3. c3 { Pawn to c3 is the same brick as the main line, just laid a move too soon — with e3 still unplayed, his queen can jump out and hit c3 and b2 together. } 3... Qb6 4. Nf3 Nc6 { His queen already sits on b6, eyeing your queenside pawns. Playing the e pawn first, as the London wants, keeps that whole square under control. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (3. Nf3 { Developing the knight before finishing the pyramid lets him crack your centre open at once. } 3... cxd4 4. c3 dxc3 { He captures on d4 and then again on c3, and you are a pawn down with nothing built to show for it. }) (3. Bxb8 { Your bishop grabs the knight on b8, but that knight was asleep at home and your bishop is the best piece you own — never make this trade. } 3... Rxb8 4. e3 c4 { You gave up the London bishop for a knight that had not even moved yet. His rook is happy on the open file. [%csl Rf4] }) (3. e4 { Striking in the centre with the e pawn before the pyramid is finished lets him win it straight back with tempo. } 3... dxe4 4. d5 Nf6 { He grabs the pawn and pushes past in the centre, and your position is a touch worse for having opened things up too soon. Build the pyramid first instead. }) 3... Nf6 { He develops the knight, and it also answers your c pawn's ambitions. Meet it with your own pawn, one square, and the pyramid stands complete. [%cal Yc2c3] } 4. c3 { The second brick goes down. Meet his c pawn with yours here, and never capture on c5. [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3][%cal Rd4c5] } (4. Nf3 { Also good, developing the knight before laying the last brick. } 4... e6 5. c3 Bd6 { The position transposes back to our plan. Building the pyramid first simply keeps the move order clearer. }) (4. Nd2 { The knight heads to d2 a move early. Playable, though it leaves his queen a poke at your b pawn later. } 4... Qb6 5. Ngf3 e6 { His queen is already out and your b pawn needs watching. Laying the last brick with the pawn keeps everything defended at once. }) (4. dxc5 { Capturing on c5 again hands him a free developing move — his bishop simply recaptures there. } 4... e6 5. Nf3 Bxc5 { He develops his bishop to c5 with tempo, and the central tension you were holding has gone for nothing. }) (4. Nc3 { His knight claims c3, the square your own c pawn was aiming for. } 4... cxd4 5. exd4 Bg4 { With that square gone, the whole pyramid plan is off the table for this game. }) 4... e6 { He shuts his own bishop in behind the e pawn. Now bring out the queen's knight — it goes to d2, never c3, so it stays out of the pyramid's way. [%cal Yb1d2] } 5. Nd2 { The queen's knight comes first. Keeping f3 open for the other knight is exactly why the order matters here. [%csl Gd2] } (5. Nf3 { Just as natural by the numbers, though it costs you the f3 square the other knight wanted. } 5... Bd6 6. Bb5+ Nc6 { Fine chess either way, but now your other knight has nowhere as useful to land. }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop moves before the knights, and it invites an immediate reply. } 5... Qb6 6. Qc2 Nh5 { His knight jumps out and pokes at your best piece before you are ready for it. [%cal Rh5f4] }) (5. Be2 { A modest square that does nothing for the pyramid plan. } 5... Bd6 6. Nf3 O-O { Level chess, and sending the knight to d2 first keeps the whole plan alive instead. }) (5. Bg3 { Stepping the bishop back before anyone has even asked it to move. } 5... Ne4 6. Nd2 Nxg3 { He simply trades off your proud bishop for nothing in return. [%csl Rg3] }) 5... Nc6 { Your knight comes out and adds pressure of its own. Now the king's knight goes to f3, the square the other one would have taken. [%cal Yg1f3] } 6. Ngf3 { The king's knight arrives too. Every piece is now heading for the middle of the board. [%csl Gf3] } (6. Bd3 { The bishop develops first, and it lets him release the central tension exactly when he wants to. } 6... Bd6 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { He is comfortable now, and your knight is still sitting at home. }) (6. Bg3 { Stepping back before the question has even been asked. } 6... Be7 7. Ngf3 Nh5 { He pokes at the bishop anyway, and you have lost a whole move for nothing. }) (6. Be2 { Quiet, but the knight was the piece with somewhere more useful to go. } 6... Bd6 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { Level chess, and your whole plan has slowed right down for it. }) (6. h3 { A little luft for the king, played at exactly the wrong moment. } 6... Bd6 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 { He offers the trade of queens and you have no good way to decline it. }) 6... Bd6 { The bishops face off. Do not trade here — step your bishop back to g3 and keep the tension alive. [%cal Yf4g3] } 7. Bg3 { You keep the tension instead of trading. The pyramid now stands complete: bishop on g3, pawns on c3, d4 and e3, and both knights out and ready. [%cal Gf4g3] } (7. Bxd6 { Trading your best piece for his bishop here gives up the whole point of the London. } 7... Qxd6 8. Bb5 O-O { His queen recaptures on d6 and stands centralised, but you spent your best bishop just to plant a pin on his knight. }) (7. Ne5 { The knight jumps to e5 too soon — there is no pawn yet to back it up, and he simply kicks it away. } 7... Qc7 8. Nxc6 Bxf4 { Your bishop is traded for his, material stays level, and your knight sits deep on c6 eyeing a7. Playable, but far messier than simply stepping the bishop back to g3. }) (7. dxc5 { Capturing on c5 gives away the whole pyramid, and the pyramid was the entire plan. } 7... Bxc5 8. Bd3 O-O { Playable, but everything the pyramid was built for has gone. }) (7. Bb5 { A pin from the far side, while your own bishop on g3 is the piece actually under attack. } 7... Bxf4 8. exf4 Qb6 { He trades off your best piece with tempo, and your pawn structure is left looking ugly. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: never let him play e5"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/VtRh8soC"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 5 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R_b_KQkq_-_5_7"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid stands: bishop on g3, pawns on c3, d4 and e3. Now watch what he does with his own king. }
7... O-O { He is safe behind his own pawns. Finish your development and point a piece straight at that king while you do it. } 8. Bd3 { The last minor piece comes out, and it stares straight down the long diagonal at h7, next to his king. [%cal Gd3h7] } (8. Bb5 { A pin on the queenside instead. Playable, but nothing of his king lives over there for it to bite on. } 8... Be7 9. Qe2 c4 { His pawn grabs space with tempo, and your bishop is stuck aiming at the wrong side of the board. }) (8. Be2 { Modest and safe, though it gives up the h7 diagonal that your bishop wanted for the attack. } 8... b6 9. O-O Bb7 { Level chess, and that bishop of yours would rather have been staring at h7. }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps to e5 a move too early — nothing backs it up yet, and he simply trades it off. } 8... Bxe5 9. dxe5 Nd7 { He trades the knight and your centre pawn on d4 is left loose behind it. }) (8. Qc2 { Your queen sits on c2, but that is the square your own bishop wanted for its retreat. } 8... b6 9. Bd3 Qc7 { You have to move the bishop again, and he gets a free developing move out of it. }) 8... Re8 { His rook backs up the e pawn's push to e5, the break that would free his whole game. Sit your knight on e5 first and deny him that. [%csl Re6] } 9. Ne5 { Plant the knight on e5 before he can free himself with e5. Nothing in this whole opening matters more than owning that square. [%csl Ge5] } (9. O-O { Castling looks natural here, but watch what he plays the moment your king tucks in. } 9... e5 10. dxe5 Nxe5 { He strikes with e5 and trades off the centre — his pieces come alive and your edge is gone. [%csl Re5] }) (9. Bxd6 { You traded on d6 instead of holding e5, and he is perfectly comfortable with the pawn push still available to him. } 9... Qxd6 10. Bb5 Bd7 { His queen is happy on d6 and nothing is stopping the e5 break he wants. }) (9. dxc5 { The central tension dissolves and he gets his pawn to e5 anyway, for nothing in return. } 9... Bxc5 10. O-O e5 { Nothing is gained by this trade, and he still gets the freer position with his pawn on e5. }) (9. Bb5 { A pin instead of holding the outpost — he does not even need to answer it. } 9... Bxg3 10. hxg3 Qb6 { Your best minor piece is gone from the board, and the e5 square still belongs to him. }) 9... Qc7 { He piles his queen up against your knight on e5. Do not retreat it — feed it another defender instead. } 10. f4 { The stonewall completes: pawns on c3, d4, e3 and f4 all standing, and your knight on e5 is now the piece to go hunting with. [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3,Gf4] } (10. Nxc6 { Trading the proud knight on e5 is also good, and it leads to a quieter sort of game. } 10... Qxc6 11. Nf3 b6 { Perfectly pleasant for him, though the pawn wall you keep instead is the whole dream of this opening. }) (10. Ndf3 { Propping the knight with a second knight instead of the pawn. Also playable. } 10... Bd7 11. a4 h6 { Solid enough, though the pawn to f4 makes for a far more menacing knight than the piece move does. }) (10. O-O { Castling now lets his knight simply take your outpost on e5 for free. } 10... Nxe5 11. dxe5 Bxe5 { He wins back the pawn and your whole plan along with it. [%cal Rc6e5] }) (10. Bb5 { A pin when the knight on e5 needed feeding instead. } 10... Bd7 11. Nxd7 Nxd7 { You helped him untangle his pieces, and the outpost on e5 is gone for good. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he grabs space with c4"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/OZufLPbi"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 5 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R_b_KQkq_-_5_7"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid is up again, and this time he grabs space on the queenside instead of castling calmly. Space like this can be a trap. }
7... c4 { His pawn to c4 locks the queenside, but from now on that pawn can never capture anything again. [%csl Rc4] } 8. b3 { Ask the question of his pawn chain at once: push to b3 and strike at its base on c4. A chain only holds as long as its base does. [%cal Gb3c4] } (8. Be2 { Quiet development instead of striking the base. Also good, but it hands him a free move to prop the chain up. } 8... O-O 9. O-O b5 { He props the base with his own b pawn first. Same plan as our main line, just a tempo slower for him. }) (8. Qc2 { Your queen is useful on c2, and the b pawn push is still coming either way. } 8... b5 9. Be2 O-O { Also fine — castling first and hitting the base with b3 right after is simply the sharper move order. }) (8. a4 { The other wing pawn instead. Just as good, and only a shade less direct than striking with the b pawn. } 8... O-O 9. Be2 b6 { Equally pleasant for him — both his queenside pawns end up joining the chain either way. }) (8. Bxd6 { Trading the bishop off before you have to. Do the trade only once it actually helps you. } 8... Qxd6 9. Be2 b5 { His queen recaptures happily on d6, and his pawn chain on the queenside is still untouched. }) 8... b5 { He props the c4 pawn with b5. Now two of his pawns are fixed on light squares, the very colour your dark-squared bishop attacks. } 9. Bxd6 { Now the trade is right: that dark-squared bishop was his best piece, and his queenside pawns are fixed where your bishop wants them. [%cal Gg3d6] } (9. a4 { The other wing pawn first instead. Exactly as good — just the same plan in the other order. } 9... b4 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 { His queen recaptures on d6. It is the very same position either way, whichever pawn moved first. }) (9. Be2 { Quiet development, but it lets him finish organising his queenside chain in peace. } 9... O-O 10. O-O Bb7 { Level chess — that trade on d6 was the move that actually improved your position. }) (9. Ne5 { The outpost on e5 instead of striking the chain, but here his own pawns simply chase your knight straight off it. } 9... Ne7 10. Qc2 a6 { The knight has no pawn behind it to hold e5, and the whole outpost plan stalls. }) (9. bxc4 { Releasing the tension on c4 yourself lets his pawn sit there in comfort instead of under pressure. } 9... bxc4 10. e4 Nxe4 { You opened the e file for him and handed back the very pawn you were pressing. Count it: he now has seven pawns to your six. }) 9... Qxd6 { His queen recaptures on d6. Now bring the second wing pawn into the attack on his chain. } 10. a4 { Two of your pawns now aim at his lone one on b5, and the pressure on that chain is real right now. [%cal Ga4b5] } (10. Qc2 { Solid, but slow — it gives his chain time to settle instead of striking it at once. } 10... O-O 11. a4 cxb3 { You end up playing the capture anyway, just a full move behind where the main line already was. }) (10. Be2 { Developing a piece when the pawns were the ones doing the real work against his chain. } 10... O-O 11. a4 b4 { Fine enough, but you handed him a free move to advance his own pawn instead. }) (10. Qc1 { The queen shuffles sideways while his queenside chain sits there waiting for you to strike it. } 10... Rb8 11. a4 a6 { He gets organised first, for far less than the pressure the main line was already applying. }) (10. a3 { The little pawn push does not attack anything on the board at all. } 10... e5 11. dxe5 Nxe5 { He breaks in the centre with a capture while you were still fiddling on the wing. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he trades on g3"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/8eMnToq5"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 5 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R_b_KQkq_-_5_7"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid is up, bishop on g3 included. If he trades it off, recapture with the right pawn and you come out the happy one. }
7... Bxg3 { His bishop takes yours on g3. Two pawns can recapture there — which one, and for what reason? } 8. hxg3 { The h pawn recaptures, heading toward the centre, and it swings your rook onto the open h file straight at his king. [%cal Gh1h8] } (8. fxg3 { The wrong pawn recaptures here: your king loses its shelter and your rook on h1 still has no open file to use. } 8... Ng4 9. Qe2 Qc7 { His knight jumps straight into the hole that pawn left on g4. [%csl Rg4] }) (8. dxc5 { Forgetting to recapture at all and grabbing a pawn instead. Count the pieces on the board before you decide that is worth it. } 8... Bc7 9. b4 O-O { Your bishop is simply still hanging on g3, uncaptured. You are down a whole piece for nothing. [%csl Rg3] }) 8... Qb6 { His queen goes after your b pawn while your own bishop is still under attack. Guard the pawn and keep building. [%cal Gb6b2] } 9. Qc2 { Your queen covers b2 from c2 and already eyes the long diagonal toward his king. Everything you own still holds. [%cal Gc2b2] } (9. Qb3 { Facing his queen down with your own instead. Just as good, and it offers to trade the queens off. } 9... O-O 10. Be2 Ng4 { Equal in value — keeping the queens on the board is simply more fun to play. }) (9. Rb1 { The rook defends b2 instead of the queen. It works, though it is a passive way to spend the move. } 9... h6 10. Bd3 Ng4 { Solid but sleepy — the queen move does the same defensive job with far more activity. }) (9. b3 { You hold the b2 pawn, but this puts a second pawn on a light square your own dark bishop can never help. } 9... h6 10. Ne5 cxd4 { Playable, though your queenside pawns end up riddled with holes for a bishop like his to work on later. }) (9. dxc5 { Grabbing the pawn on c5 and leaving b2 to whatever comes. His queen simply takes it. } 9... Qxb2 10. c4 O-O { He takes the b2 pawn and your whole queenside structure falls apart around the hole it leaves. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (9. Qc1 { The queen retreats all the way to the corner and does nothing useful there at all. } 9... cxd4 10. exd4 Ng4 { He breaks into your position first with the knight, and you are still busy untangling your own pieces. }) 1-0


