[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid is already built. If he brings a knight out early, watch what his queen does next. }
4... Nc6 { He develops his knight. Answer with the piece that also guards your b pawn. } 5. Nd2 { The queen's knight goes to d2. In a moment you will see exactly why that square matters. [%csl Gd2] } (5. Nf3 { The other knight instead, and it leaves your b pawn with nobody watching it. } 5... Nh5 6. dxc5 Nxf4 7. exf4 e6 { He trades off your best piece for it. Playable, though d2 was the square that actually mattered here. }) (5. a4 { A wing pawn move at the moment your pieces should be coming out instead. } 5... e6 6. Nf3 Nh5 { He pokes at your bishop and you have gained nothing at all for the tempo spent. }) (5. Be2 { Quiet development, but it leaves your queenside completely unguarded. } 5... Qb6 6. Qc1 cxd4 { Your queen has to run all the way to the corner just to defend it. }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop is fine on d3, but b2 is left loose right behind it. } 5... Qb6 6. b3 Bg4 { You had to weaken your own queenside just to hold on to that pawn. }) 5... Qb6 { There is his queen, eyeing b2. Now you can see exactly why the knight went to d2 first. } 6. Qb3 { Queen faces queen head on. Neither can safely take, so nothing happens on the board for a move. [%cal Gb3b6] } (6. Qc2 { Also a defence, but the queen on c2 does not stare him down the way the one on b3 does. } 6... Nh5 7. Be5 cxd4 { He trades off your bishop and ends up the busier piece on the board. }) (6. Ngf3 { Developing a knight while leaving the b pawn to whatever comes for it. } 6... Qxb2 7. Rb1 Qxc3 { His queen helps herself to two pawns, on b2 and then c3. You have some activity for it, not enough. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (6. Bd3 { The bishop comes out while his queen simply walks into your queenside. } 6... Qxb2 7. Ne2 cxd4 { A pawn falls for free on d4, and your whole position is left loose. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (6. Qc1 { Hiding the queen in the corner. It holds the pawn, and it does nothing else useful. } 6... cxd4 7. exd4 e5 { He breaks open the centre with e5 while your queen sits there doing nothing. }) 6... c4 { His pawn grabs space on c4 with tempo, but that pawn has stopped attacking anything at all now. [%csl Rc4] } 7. Qc2 { Step your queen aside instead and keep the queens on the board — trading here would only help him untangle. [%csl Gc2] } (7. Qxb6 { Never trade queens here. Look what the recapture does for his rook and his pawns. } 7... axb6 8. a3 b5 { His rook wakes up on the open a file, and his doubled pawns turn into a real wall. }) (7. Nxc4 { That c4 pawn looks poisoned, but count its defenders before you decide not to take it. } 7... dxc4 8. Bxc4 e6 { His pawn recaptures your knight, and your bishop recaptures the pawn behind it — a knight for two pawns, and you are clearly worse for it. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (7. Bxc4 { The other capture, and it costs you a piece in exactly the same way as the knight did. } 7... dxc4 8. Nxc4 Qa6 { His pawn recaptures your bishop, and your knight retakes the pawn — a bishop for two pawns, and his queen and knights are simply the livelier army now. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (7. Qd1 { Retreating the queen all the way home hands over the b pawn for nothing at all. } 7... Qxb2 8. Ne2 Bf5 { His queen eats the pawn and sits perfectly safe on b2. You are worse for letting her in. }) 7... g6 { He heads for the fianchetto instead. Meanwhile the central break you have been building sits there ready to use. } 8. e4 { There it is: his c pawn has already gone past d4, so the middle of the board belongs to you now. [%cal Ge4d5] } (8. h3 { Solid and slow, but the whole reason you let his pawn go past was to seize the centre with this very break. } 8... Bg7 9. Ngf3 O-O { Level chess, and you had the central break sitting right there and let it pass. }) (8. Be2 { Developing quietly at the moment the central break was calling out to be played. } 8... Bg7 9. h3 Bf5 { Equal chess. His pieces come out freely and the moment for the break is gone. }) (8. Bg3 { Tucking the bishop away for no reason at all, with nothing chasing it yet. } 8... Bf5 9. Qc1 Nh5 { He chases the bishop anyway, and you are left a touch worse for the wasted move. }) (8. Ngf3 { Natural development, but it lets him poke at your bishop first instead of you striking the centre. } 8... Nh5 9. Bg3 Bf5 { The central break has slipped away, and he sits perfectly comfortable. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he goes for the fianchetto"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/mhcNK56U"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_1"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The queen pawn is out, and he can answer it many ways. Keep going regardless: the bishop still comes out to f4. }
1... Nf6 { He brings the knight out first this time instead of a pawn. Your plan does not change at all. } 2. Bf4 { The London bishop again, outside the pawn chain before the pawns can shut it in. [%csl Gf4] } (2. c4 { The Queen's Gambit, a beautiful opening in its own right and a completely different plan from ours. } 2... e6 3. g3 d5 { Marvellous chess from both sides, but we are learning the London System today, not the Queen's Gambit. }) (2. Nf3 { Also excellent, and also a different opening from the one we are building. } 2... d5 3. c4 e6 { Fine chess either way, but the bishop already out on f4 is what actually makes this the London. }) (2. e3 { Playing the e pawn before the bishop shuts your own bishop in behind it again. } 2... d5 3. Nf3 e6 { That trapped bishop is exactly the trouble the London plan exists to avoid. [%csl Rc1] }) (2. Bg5 { The other bishop square instead. Playable, though he can chase it away with tempo. } 2... d5 3. e3 c5 { His pawn chases the bishop and trades it for a knight, and the whole London idea is gone with it. }) 2... g6 { His bishop is heading to g7 to fianchetto. Against this set-up, forget the pyramid plan entirely. } 3. Nc3 { The knight goes to c3, not the c pawn, so your e pawn stays free to jump forward later. [%csl Gc3] } (3. e3 { Quiet and solid, but passive against a fianchetto — his bishop on g7 gets to breathe freely. } 3... Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O { Playable. Against the fianchetto you want the bigger pawn centre instead of the usual pyramid. }) (3. c4 { A fine and very popular opening in its own right, and simply not the London we are learning. } 3... c5 4. d5 b5 { Good chess from him, but this is no longer the London once the c pawn leads the way. }) (3. Nd2 { The other knight square. Also good, and a shade more passive than sending the knight to c3. } 3... c5 4. e3 Bg7 { Equal chess either way — the knight on c3 is the one that actually backs the e pawn's later jump. }) (3. Nf3 { Natural development, though it does nothing to help the e pawn's later jump forward. } 3... Bg7 4. Nc3 d5 { You reach the same set-up anyway, just one move slower than sending the knight to c3 first. }) 3... d5 { He plants his own pawn on d5 and stops your e pawn dead. So the attack now comes down the other wing instead. } 4. e3 { Quietly solid, and it frees the rook pawn to run all the way to h5 and beyond. [%csl Ge3] } (4. Nf3 { Also good, and just as natural a way to reach the same position. } 4... Bg7 5. e3 O-O { The very same position either way, whichever piece moves first — both orders are fine. }) (4. Nb5 { The knight's raid to b5 gains very little here. } 4... Na6 5. Nc3 Nh5 { His knight on a6 already guards the queenside, so your knight has to come home again for nothing. }) (4. h4 { The storm before you even have a square ready for your own king. } 4... Bg7 5. e3 h5 { He stops the pawn cold and leaves a hole behind on g4 for you to use instead. }) (4. h3 { A quiet move that prepares nothing at all against his fianchetto. } 4... Bg7 5. Nb5 Na6 { Level chess — pushing the e pawn was the move that actually mattered here. }) 4... Bg7 { His bishop fianchettoes to g7, eyeing your queenside from a distance. Against this set-up, start the kingside pawn storm instead of the pyramid. } 5. h4 { The rook pawn charges forward. Bold rather than calm, and very hard for him to meet over the board. [%cal Gh4h5] } (5. Nf3 { Calm development instead, and perfectly respectable chess. } 5... O-O 6. Be2 c5 { A small edge and no fireworks. We came here for the storm, not a quiet edge. }) (5. Be2 { Solid and quiet, and just as good by the numbers as charging the rook pawn. } 5... O-O 6. Nf3 c5 { Equal chess, and the rook pawn simply asks him a much harder question to answer. }) (5. Nb5 { The knight raid again, and again he simply guards against it with ease. } 5... Na6 6. Nc3 Nb8 { He shuffles the knight back home, and so do you. Nothing at all is gained by either side. }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop lands on the wrong diagonal to trouble a fianchetto set-up. } 5... O-O 6. h3 c5 { His bishop on g7 is doing more work than yours now. }) 5... O-O { He castles his king straight into the coming storm. Keep the pawn charging. } 6. h5 { The rook pawn reaches h5. He really should have left that pawn alone rather than take it next. } (6. Nb5 { The knight raid instead of pushing the pawn. Also playable, if less thematic here. } 6... Na6 7. Nc3 c5 { Equal chess. You already had a real storm going — better to finish it than switch plans. }) (6. Nf3 { Developing a piece and letting the attack go cold at exactly the wrong moment. } 6... c5 7. Ne5 Qa5 { He strikes back in the centre, and your pawn on h4 is left loose behind the fight. }) (6. Be2 { Quiet, and now he stops your h pawn himself before it can do any damage. } 6... h5 7. Nf3 c5 { The whole storm has gone. Level chess, and nothing left to show for the plan. }) (6. Qd2 { The queen comes out before the pawn storm has actually done its job. } 6... c5 7. O-O-O Qa5 { He counters on the very wing where your own king is about to castle. }) 6... Nxh5 { He grabs the pawn on h5, and that was the mistake — playing c5 instead would have kept everything solid. Now make him pay for taking it. } 7. Rxh5 { The rook swings in on h5. Give up the exchange here and rip his king's shelter wide open. [%cal Gh5h7] } (7. Be5 { Backing the bishop off the h-file gives up the entire point of pushing the rook pawn in the first place. } 7... Nf6 8. f3 Nbd7 { He is up a clean pawn and perfectly safe. You are worse for having backed down. }) (7. Bg5 { A pin that achieves nothing while he sits a pawn ahead. } 7... c5 8. Be2 Nf6 { The knight simply walks home to safety, and you have nothing at all to show for the storm. }) (7. Qf3 { Aiming the queen at the knight, but he simply trades it off. } 7... Nxf4 8. Qxf4 c6 { Your best piece is gone from the board, and so is the pawn you were chasing. }) (7. Bh2 { Tucking the bishop away while a whole pawn is still missing from your side. } 7... c5 8. g4 cxd4 { He breaks open the centre and you are left worse, the pawn storm wasted. }) 7... gxh5 { He has no choice but to recapture the rook. Now bring the queen in to finish the job. } 8. Qxh5 { You have given up the exchange for this: his king stands with no pawns left around it, and every piece you own still points straight at it. [%csl Rg8] } (8. Qf3 { The wrong square for the queen. He develops with tempo and you are suddenly the one in trouble. } 8... Bg4 9. Qxd5 Nc6 { His bishop comes out with gain of time, and your queen is the piece getting chased now. }) (8. Bd3 { You never recaptured the pawn, and his king simply gets safe again with nothing to show for the sacrifice. } 8... Bg4 9. f3 Bc8 { A whole exchange spent for nothing at all — his king walks away untouched. }) (8. Nf3 { Developing a piece at the exact moment his king was one move from falling. } 8... Bg4 9. Qd2 f6 { The attack has stopped completely, and you are still down the exchange for it. }) (8. Be5 { The bishop instead of the queen is far too slow to keep the attack alive. } 8... Bg4 9. Be2 f6 { He kicks every one of your attacking pieces away, and you are left with nothing for the sacrifice. }) 1-0


