{ The pyramid's bishop is already out on f4. If he copies the idea with his own bishop, the bill arrives on b7. }
2... Bf5 { He mirrors your bishop move, but his own bishop has just left the b pawn with nobody to guard it. [%csl Rb7] } 3. c4 { Strike in the centre with the c pawn while his queenside sits loose. This is the sharp way to punish the copy. [%cal Gc4d5] } (3. e3 { The safe way instead, and a perfectly respected one too. } 3... e6 4. c4 Nf6 { Solid and pleasant for him, but there is nothing yet for your pieces to bite on. }) (3. Nf3 { The same idea a move later. Also perfectly good chess. } 3... e6 4. c4 Nf6 { He reaches the same position anyway — the c pawn can simply lead the way first instead. }) (3. Nd2 { Quiet development while his queenside pawn still sits there completely loose. } 3... e6 4. e3 Nf6 { Level chess, and you turned down the sharp line that actually punishes the copycat bishop. }) (3. Nc3 { The knight claims c3, the very square your c pawn wanted for its own strike. } 3... e6 4. e3 Nf6 { Solid enough, but your sharpest plan against the copycat bishop has gone. }) 3... e6 { He props up his centre with the e pawn. Develop your own pieces and keep piling pressure on his queenside. } 4. Nc3 { Every piece you add now points straight at his loose queenside pawns. [%csl Gc3] } (4. cxd5 { Releasing the central tension and letting him get comfortably solid. } 4... exd5 5. Nc3 Nf6 { Playable, though the pressure you had built on his queenside is gone now. }) (4. Nf3 { Also good, and a shade quieter a way to develop. } 4... dxc4 5. Nbd2 Nf6 { Fine chess — the queen's knight is the one that actually belongs on c3 for this plan. }) (4. e3 { Quiet. His b pawn is safe again and the whole point of the plan has slipped away. } 4... Nf6 5. Nd2 c5 { Level, and he sits comfortably now that the pressure has eased. }) (4. Qa4+ { A check that only helps him — he simply blocks it and develops a piece for free. } 4... Nd7 5. Nc3 Nf6 { He blocks the check and develops at the same time. You have gained nothing from it. }) 4... Nf6 { He develops his last knight. Now the move this whole line was built to reach. } 5. Qb3 { Your queen aims at b7 and d5 at the same time, and he has no comfortable way to answer both. [%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] } (5. Nf3 { Sensible and good, but it lets him consolidate and protect the pawn calmly. } 5... Nbd7 6. c5 c6 { Pleasant for him, and the queen sortie to b3 is the entire point of punishing the copycat bishop. }) (5. e3 { You let him off the hook here — the b pawn is covered and he stands fine. } 5... Bb4 6. Nf3 O-O { Level chess, and the copycat bishop has gotten away with it after all. }) (5. Rc1 { The rook lines up on the c file, but that file is not open yet for it to use. } 5... dxc4 6. e3 Nd5 { His knight centralises on d5, attacking your bishop on f4, and the pawn he grabbed earlier on c4 stays banked. Your rook still has no open file to work with. }) (5. c5 { Locking the centre with the pawn push and letting him breathe easily. } 5... Be7 6. e3 O-O { The central tension is gone, and so is the edge you had against the copycat bishop. }) 1-0


