{ The pyramid's bishop is already out on f4. If he strikes at your centre with the c pawn, do not capture it. }
2... c5 { He hits your d pawn. Taking it hands him the centre for free. Think about what the pawn wants to do instead. } 3. d5 { Push the pawn past him to d5. Now his pawn on c5 bites on nothing at all. [%csl Gd5] } (3. e3 { Playable, but once he plays c5, that pawn wants to run forward, not sit and be propped up. } 3... d6 4. Nc3 Qb6 { His queen pokes at your queenside from b6. Playable, but pushing the pawn to d5 was the stronger answer. }) (3. dxc5 { He wins the pawn straight back with tempo, and you have nothing at all to show for the detour. } 3... Na6 4. f3 Nxc5 { His knight lands on a great square with tempo, hitting your bishop on f4. }) (3. c3 { Propping a pawn that actually wanted to run forward instead of sit still. } 3... Qb6 4. Nd2 cxd4 { He trades on d4 and hits your b pawn in the same move. Your set-up is left tangled. }) (3. Nc3 { Developing a piece while the centre is still completely undecided. } 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight gains a tempo on your queen for free. }) 3... b5 { The wing thrust with the b pawn. Do not chase it — answer in the centre instead, where the real fight is. } 4. e4 { Push straight down the middle with the e pawn. His pawn on b5 is miles from where the fighting actually happens. [%csl Ge4] } (4. Nf3 { Solid development, and also a perfectly good answer. } 4... Bb7 5. c4 d6 { Perfectly fine for him, but the pawn to e4 is simply the stronger central strike. }) (4. f3 { Propping the e4 pawn with f3 before playing it. Also playable, just one move slower. } 4... g6 5. e4 Bg7 { He reaches the same set-up anyway, just a move slower than meeting e4 head on. }) (4. c4 { Grabbing space with the c pawn is playable, but he gets the open lines he paid a tempo for. } 4... b4 5. Nf3 g6 { His pawn cramps your position, and the queenside opens up in his favour for it. }) (4. e3 { The little pawn push when the bigger central strike with e4 was sitting right there. } 4... Qa5+ 5. c3 Bb7 { His queen checks on a5 first, costing you the move to block with c3, and your centre pawn is left on e3 instead of the bigger e4 push. }) 4... Nxe4 { He grabs the pawn on e4. Hit the knight at once and look at what lies behind it too. } 5. Qf3 { Your queen attacks the knight on e4 and stares down the long diagonal toward a8 at the same time. [%cal Gf3e4,Gf3a8] } (5. Bd3 { Also good, but the bishop on d3 only does the one job of attacking the knight. } 5... Qa5+ 6. Nd2 Nf6 { Fine, and the queen move does two jobs at once instead of just the one. }) (5. Nf3 { Developing a knight while leaving him a clean extra pawn on the board. } 5... Bb7 6. Nc3 Nxc3 { His knight grabs yours on c3 too, but your b pawn simply recaptures there and the position stays close to level — just the one pawn behind, not more. }) (5. c3 { Too slow — he keeps the extra pawn and still gets his pieces out in peace. } 5... a6 6. a4 Nf6 { He keeps the pawn and calmly untangles his pieces. Level chess, and you had a far sharper answer available. }) (5. Be2 { Quiet development with a whole pawn still missing from the board. } 5... g6 6. Bf3 Nf6 { You spent two whole moves on that bishop, and he is perfectly comfortable for it. }) 1-0


