{ The pyramid pawn already sits on d5. If his queen comes hunting for it, let her have the pawn. }
3... Qb6 { Her queen hits b2 and d5 at the same time. Do not defend the pawn — ask what is actually worth more here. [%csl Rb2] } 4. Nc3 { Ignore the pawn entirely. Every piece you bring out is worth more than that one square. [%csl Gc3] } (4. Bc1 { Crawling the bishop backwards just to save one pawn. He is delighted to see it. } 4... c4 5. Nc3 e6 { You lost three whole moves shuffling that bishop for a single pawn. }) (4. b3 { Guarding the pawn with b3 only invites her queen deeper into your own camp. } 4... Qb4+ 5. Bd2 Qd4 { Her queen sits right in the middle of your position on d4, doing real damage. }) (4. Nf3 { Developing another piece, but it leaves the knight on c3 with nobody watching it. } 4... Qxb2 5. Be5 Qb4+ { Her queen grabs the knight and checks at the same time — the knight on c3 needed a defender first. }) (4. e4 { Building the centre with e4 while leaving your queen a free pawn to eat. } 4... Qxb2 5. Nd2 Nxe4 { She eats the pawn on b2, and his knight then grabs another pawn of yours on the very next move. }) 4... Qxb2 { She takes the pawn on b2. There is exactly one move left that defends everything at once. } 5. Bd2 { The bishop drops back to d2, covering the knight on c3 and asking her queen to go back home. [%cal Gd2c3] } (5. Be5 { That knight on c3 looks free for the taking, but do not grab it — your bishop already sits on the very same diagonal from e5 and simply takes the queen instead. } 5... Nxd5 6. Qb1 Qxb1+ { She wisely leaves the trap alone and grabs the pawn on d5 instead. The queens come off next, and you are down two pawns for it. }) (5. e4 { Pushing a centre pawn here instead of guarding the knight on c3, which her queen is attacking directly. } 5... Qxc3+ 6. Bd2 Qe5 { Her queen grabs the undefended knight on c3 with check; once the king blocks with the bishop, she retreats to e5. A whole knight is gone for nothing. [%cal Rb2c3] }) (5. f3 { The same mistake with a different pawn — the knight on c3 still has no defender, and only the bishop to d2 actually covers that square. } 5... Qxc3+ 6. Bd2 Qe5 { The knight disappears the same way. Only the bishop retreating to d2 actually stops this, whichever pawn you push instead. }) (5. Nb5 { Moving the attacked knight along the very same file the queen already commands, so it stays just as hanging on b5. } 5... Qxb5 6. f3 Qa5+ { She simply captures the knight on b5 for free and checks from a5 next. A whole piece is gone for nothing. [%cal Rb2b5] }) 5... Qb6 { She retreats her queen home. He spent three whole moves chasing one pawn; you spent them building the centre instead. } 6. e4 { Now build in earnest: two big pawns going up and a real lead in development to show for it. [%csl Ge4,Gd5] } (6. f4 { The other big central pawn instead. Just as good, and it follows the same idea. } 6... Qd8 7. e4 d6 { Equal value either way — both central pawns are coming up regardless of which leads. }) (6. Rb1 { Chasing her queen with the rook instead of building the centre. } 6... Qd8 7. f4 a6 { Also playable, but the centre pawns are worth more than chasing the queen around. }) (6. a4 { A wing pawn move when the whole point was building the centre. } 6... g6 7. e4 d6 { You build the centre pawn anyway, just a move behind where the main line already was. }) (6. Nf3 { Developing a knight when the centre pawns should be marching first. } 6... g6 7. e4 d6 { Roughly level — the pawn wanted that square pushed first, ahead of the knight. }) 6... d6 { He props up the middle with his own pawn. Add the second big pawn of your own now. } 7. f4 { Two big central pawns standing and every piece ready behind them. This is exactly what letting the pawn go bought you. [%csl Ge4,Gf4] } (7. a4 { Also good, and it grabs squares on his queenside at the same time. } 7... g6 8. f4 Bg7 { Fine for him, though the f pawn coming up next carries far more menace than this. }) (7. Bc4 { The bishop is fine on c4, but the pawn push is simply the faster plan. } 7... g6 8. f4 Bg7 { Playable for him, and you push the f pawn up anyway right after. }) (7. Rb1 { The rook again, and again the central pawns were the whole point of the plan. } 7... Qd8 8. f4 a6 { Slower for him, and he only gets organised because you paused to chase instead of push. }) (7. h4 { A wing storm on the h file while the centre still is not finished. } 7... g6 8. h5 Nxh5 { He simply captures the pawn on h5, and you have nothing at all to show for the loose storm. }) 1-0


