{ This is the hardest set-up for the pyramid to meet. One well-timed bishop move solves every problem it causes. }
3... d6 { He is about to build a wall with e5 and squeeze your bishop against it. Where does your knight belong first? } 4. Nc3 { The knight comes out to c3 first. Since the c pawn stays home, that square is free for it. [%csl Gc3] } (4. Nf3 { Natural development, but it does nothing to stop the wall he is about to build. } 4... e5 5. dxe6 Bxe6 { You had to release the centre with this capture, and he develops a piece for free off the back of it. }) (4. c4 { His pawn takes the very square your knight wanted for itself. } 4... e5 5. Bg5 Be7 { Playable, though your queen's knight has no natural home left after this. }) (4. a4 { A wing pawn move while a wall is being built to trap your bishop. } 4... e6 5. c4 e5 { That a pawn does nothing to solve the bishop's problem. Get a piece out instead. }) (4. h4 { A pawn storm before you have even solved the bishop's problem. } 4... e5 5. Bg5 Qb6 { His queen hits b2 while your bishop is still short of squares to run to. }) 4... e5 { There is the wall on e5, and your bishop is suddenly very short of squares. [%csl Rf4][%cal Re5f4] } 5. Bg5 { Not backwards, sideways: pinning his knight on f6 means it cannot help push the wall further, and your bishop was never actually trapped. [%cal Gg5f6] } (5. dxe6 { Capturing in passing on e6 is playable, but it gives up the whole clamp you were building. } 5... Bxe6 6. Nf3 Nc6 { His pieces come out with tempo and the space you had claimed is gone. }) (5. Bg3 { Retreating the bishop all the way to g3 buries it behind his own pawn wall. } 5... Qb6 6. Rb1 Qa5 { Three moves spent on that bishop, and it still does nothing useful. [%csl Rg3] }) (5. Bxe5 { Count the defenders before grabbing that pawn — his own d pawn guards the square you are about to take. } 5... dxe5 6. e4 c4 { His pawn recaptures the bishop, a whole piece given for just one pawn. You are lost. }) (5. Bc1 { All the way back home. Three moves spent on that bishop, with nothing at all to show for it. } 5... e4 6. g3 Qe7 { His pawns roll forward unchecked and your whole position is cramped. }) 1-0


