{ No knight out, no bishop out, just a pawn — no pyramid today. This position needs a completely different set-up. }
1... c5 { He hits your pawn before developing a single piece. Punish that greed. } 2. d5 { Push straight past him. He handed you the whole centre before moving a single piece. [%csl Gd5] } (2. e3 { Now it is a dull, symmetrical game, and you gave away the chance to punish him for free. } 2... d5 3. c4 e6 { Level chess, and the push past his pawn was there for the taking instead. }) (2. e4 { You grab the big centre, but he simply trades your pawn off and the position settles down. } 2... cxd4 3. Nf3 e5 { He is perfectly fine, and the extra central pawn you had is gone. }) (2. dxc5 { You can try to hold the pawn on c5, but pushing past instead gives you space for free. } 2... e6 3. e4 Bxc5 { He wins the pawn straight back and develops his bishop with tempo doing it. }) (2. c3 { Propping the pawn on c5 with c3, when it actually wanted to run straight past instead. } 2... Nf6 3. d5 e6 { You push the pawn past anyway, just a move later than doing it right away. }) 2... Nf6 { A piece finally comes out. Bring the knight, and keep the c pawn at home for it. } 3. Nc3 { That square is for a knight, not a pawn — keep c2 clear for the piece to land on. [%csl Gc3][%cal Rc2c4] } (3. c4 { The c pawn gets hit at once and the c3 square is gone for your own knight. } 3... b5 4. Nf3 b4 { His pawns come rolling at you and your knight has nowhere left to land. }) (3. Nf3 { Playable, but the queen's knight is the one that actually wants to move to c3 first. } 3... b5 4. c4 Bb7 { Fine for him, and now his own c pawn had to move anyway to make room. }) (3. Bf4 { This is the one moment the bishop must wait on c1 — he strikes at your centre before it can come out. } 3... d6 4. Nc3 e5 { The pawn wall goes up on e5 and your bishop is suddenly short of squares. }) (3. e3 { Solid, but it turns down the big centre he just handed you for free. } 3... e6 4. Nc3 exd5 { Level chess — taking the extra central space while it was on offer was the stronger choice. }) 3... g6 { He fianchettoes his bishop. Now claim the whole centre with your pawns. } 4. e4 { The full centre is yours, pawns on d5 and e4, and you stand a long way ahead already. [%csl Ge4,Gd5] } (4. Nf3 { Developing the knight first. Just as good, and it reaches the very same plan. } 4... d6 5. e4 Bg7 { The central pawn arrives either way — both move orders work just as well. }) (4. a4 { Grabbing queenside space instead. Also playable, though the centre is the bigger prize. } 4... d6 5. Nf3 Bg7 { Equal in value, but the centre pawn is still the bigger prize to claim first. }) (4. Bg5 { A pin on his knight, though nothing yet holds that knight in place to be pinned. } 4... d6 5. e4 Bg7 { You play the central pawn anyway, and his bishop may simply get chased from the pin. }) (4. Bf4 { The London bishop comes out and stares straight at his pawn wall on g6. } 4... Bg7 5. a4 Nh5 { He kicks the bishop away and you lose a tempo for it. Claiming the centre pawn was the move instead. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he plays the Dutch"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/YY3yrlXn"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
