[Role "exercise"]

{ Against the Dutch Defence, refuse him a normal game entirely. Offer a pawn instead, for a real fight. }
1... f5 { The Dutch Defence. He likely has a whole book of theory ready for it. Give him something else to think about instead. } 2. e4 { The Staunton Gambit: one pawn offered just to drag him out of his own preparation. [%cal Ge4f5] } (2. Bf4 { You can play the usual pyramid here too. It is fine, and a genuinely popular choice. } 2... e6 3. e3 Nf6 { A good game for him, and one he already knows well. The gambit is simply more fun to play. }) (2. c4 { A normal game — exactly the kind of position he was hoping to reach. } 2... Nf6 3. g3 e6 { Solid and slightly better by the numbers, but it walks straight into his own preparation. }) (2. g3 { The main line against the Dutch. Also good, and just as well known to him. } 2... e6 3. Bg2 Nf6 { A long theoretical fight either way. The gambit sidesteps all of that memorised theory. }) (2. Bg5 { A sharp try, and a well-respected one in its own right. } 2... h6 3. Bh4 g5 { Strong too, and it wins a pawn back at once. The gambit's pawn offer is still our chosen weapon here. }) (2. Nc3 { Also strong, and probably the most testing reply of them all. } 2... Nf6 3. Bg5 d5 { A pleasant edge for you, but he is still playing straight out of his own book. }) (2. Nf3 { Quiet development. Perfectly good, and a completely normal way to meet the Dutch. } 2... e6 3. g3 Be7 { Better by the numbers, and exactly the sort of game he was hoping to reach. }) 2... fxe4 { He takes the pawn. Do not rush to win it back right away. } 3. Nc3 { Bring your pieces out instead and let him do the worrying over the extra pawn. [%csl Gc3] } (3. f3 { Grabbing the pawn back at once is playable, but pinning the knight first keeps more pressure on. } 3... exf3 4. Nxf3 Nf6 { Level chess — bringing the knight out first keeps far more pressure on his position. }) (3. Bg5 { The bishop before the knight. Also playable, in a different move order. } 3... d5 4. c4 Nf6 { Equal chess — the knight coming first also covers the pawn's escape square on e4. }) (3. Bf4 { The London bishop, but here on f4 it is simply the wrong square for this position. } 3... d5 4. f3 Nf6 { He builds a solid pawn wall and keeps the extra pawn comfortably. }) (3. c4 { Grabbing more space with a pawn move while a whole pawn is still missing from the board. } 3... e6 4. Nc3 Nf6 { He sits a clean pawn up and perfectly solid. You are worse for it. }) 3... Nf6 { Bringing the knight out is the only genuinely good move he has here. Now pin it. } 4. Bg5 { Pin the very piece guarding his extra pawn — it has nowhere to run from that pin. [%cal Gg5f6] } (4. f3 { Grabbing the pawn back straight away is playable, but the pin keeps more pressure on. } 4... d5 5. fxe4 dxe4 { Level chess — he simply holds the pawn on e4 in comfort this way. }) (4. Be3 { The bishop does nothing useful there. It belongs on g5, pinning the knight. } 4... e6 5. a3 d5 { He builds his own wall and keeps the extra pawn safely tucked away. }) (4. Bf4 { The wrong diagonal for this plan: the knight, not the king, was the piece worth pinning. } 4... e6 5. f3 Bb4 { He develops with a pin of his own in return. You are worse for the wasted move. }) (4. a3 { A quiet move with a whole pawn still missing from the board. } 4... d5 5. f3 Nc6 { He calmly consolidates, and you have nothing at all to show for the pawn. }) 4... g6 { He fianchettoes his bishop, giving it a home. Finish your own development now. } 5. Bc4 { Out comes the bishop, pointing straight at f7. The position is roughly balanced, and it is a real fight either way. [%cal Gc4f7] } (5. h4 { Sharp, and about equal too, though getting a piece out first is the calmer road. } 5... d5 6. h5 Bf5 { Also playable. Development stays the safer road compared with the pawn storm. }) (5. d5 { Space instead of pressure with this pawn push. Also equal, and perfectly sound. } 5... d6 6. Nge2 Bg7 { Fine for him — the bishop sitting on c4 already asks the sharper question of the two. }) (5. f3 { Fine, but a shade worse than simply developing. That pawn on e4 is not running anywhere. } 5... d5 6. Qd2 Bg7 { Level chess. Getting your pieces out first is still the more useful plan. }) (5. Bxf6 { Trading off your good bishop here for no reason at all. } 5... exf6 6. Nxe4 d5 { He wins the pawn back and your attacking pieces have nothing left to show for it. }) 1-0
